hey everyone how's it going hope you are  well so today we're going to talk about  how I make my career decisions in tech  speaking of making decisions in a tech  career I have a brand new podcast in  case L haven't heard it's called if-else  and I am so so so excited to share with  you all in each episode we meet a  software developer who's at a crossroads  either they need to make a technical  decision or a career when they're at an  if-else choice if you will and we call  in experts and people from the industry  to help them make that decision I am the  host of this podcast and I am so excited  to share with you all about it because  it's genuinely something I am so  interested in and really want to do more  of in helping people to make these tough  career decisions in tech the podcast is  brought to you by CTO AI they are the  makers of the ops platform and I've been  partnering with them on season 1 of this  podcast and it's been so fun working  with them on this we have three episodes  out right now so you can listen to them  wherever you listen to your podcast  there is a link in the description box  down below so please check it out and I  really hope you enjoy so on this topic  of decision-making I wanted to create a  video about making career decisions  there is going to be a video in the  future about making technical decisions  which i think is a bit of a different  topic than this one so today we're going  to talk about career stuff and what I  mean career decision things like what  company are you going to work for what  job were you going to do what area of  tech are you going to be in sometimes  what city are you going to live in as  well the advice that I'm going to give  is all based on my own personal  experiences they're not prescriptive  there's a lot of other ways to make  these decisions and if there's anything  that I don't mention I would love for  you all to leave comments down below to  let me know how you all make career  decisions I do know for a fact that  there are lots of software engineers who  watch my videos who can give a lot of  their own insight and have gone through  lots of different paths and journeys  than I have and I would love to hear  from y'all so make sure to let me know  what you think about this topic too so  before we dive into the how I kind of  wanted to dive into the why of career  decisions and I think one of the  foundational things that I learned on  early in my career around career ladders  and journeys and stuff is that it's not  actually a ladder I read somewhere once  in my first year of working that instead  of thinking about a ladder where there's  only one way up and one way down think  about it in terms of like a jungle gym  where there's lots of ways up and  there's lots of ways down and at the end  of the day you might reach a different  destination than someone who started off  at the same place that you're at which I  think conceptually makes a lot of sense  because life is so different from person  to person and the opportunities and  experiences that you're offered are  always going to differ and everything is  serendipitous there's no way of knowing  everything is just ambiguous and who  knows what's gonna happen in the future  and what has happened in the past so if  we're going on this career jungle-gym  sort of analogy then you can imagine  that the next step that you take from  your first one is going to affect what  opportunities you have after that as  well I've also heard this whole jungle  gym analogy talked about in terms of  doors where you maybe have a set of  doors in front of you and then you  choose one and then when you go through  that door there's another set of doors  that are presented to you because you  chose that one door and that is just  kind of all to talk about how whatever  decision that you make is going to open  new doors and it's going to really set  you off on a journey toward some  direction in life and your career  basically this is a really complicated  way of saying that there is no one path  or one journey or right or wrong way to  have a career in tech it's just based on  how you make decisions and how you want  to lead your career but I do want to  really hone in on the fact that whatever  decisions you make today will affect  what decisions you make tomorrow five  years from now and ten years from now no  pressure but I mean like you said  there's no right or wrong  so there's no incorrect answer there's  no correct answer it's really just based  on how life reveals itself to you and I  mean I think it's also safe to say that  a career is a really big important part  of your life especially if you're going  to work full-time you're just gonna  spend a lot of time working in your life  and a lot of people derive their sense  of fulfillment and their sense of like  self in their work and that's totally  fine and also if you don't that's also  totally fine but I think it's something  you know career along with so many other  things that are worth giving this level  of thought to for how you want to  navigate it the other important factor  in all this which makes it kind of even  more important to think about and be  really intentional about is the fact  that the tech industry moves super super  quickly especially if you work in parts  of the tech industry that are like  product oriented I feel like the  industry reinvents itself every two to  five years in terms of what technologies  are commonly used what companies exist  out there who are the people working on  this stuff and so I think because of  that it's really important to be nimble  and make decisions that feel well  informed and are going to bring you to  where ever you want to go in your career  so let's get into what are some things  that I think about when making a career  decision and generally there's three  things for me the first thing and that  is kind of a prerequisite to all this  for me to consider any opportunity is  are my needs met this thing is  definitely balanced by the other two  points that I will get into but  basically it's just are my needs met am  I getting paid enough  do I have housing am I going to have  health care these are all really  important things for me in order for me  to do my best to work in a tech career  and tech careers are really demanding  and I need a lot of these things in  order to be myself and feel myself and  do good work and I know that I'm really  fortunate to be in this position and I'm  really privileged to be able to ask for  these things but it's given me a lot of  time to think about what I need in  life in order for me to live basically  and I think that I can the fact that I  can ask for these things means that I  should ask for them and I will ask for  them I mean ultimately it's also a lot  about like how much does the company  value me what are they willing to give  me in exchange for what work I do and  that has to be fair in some way and  whatever dollar value whatever else gets  put on it it has to feel mutual to me  because I want me to want them as much  as they want to need me and there has to  be some exchange of services and goods  there for it to work out so that's  number one just are my needs met like do  I have everything that I need in order  to do good work number two is related to  the hold or is in Jungle Jim analogy  which is what opportunities does this  open up for me and this requires a lot  of thinking on my part for what are some  of the opportunities that I need to gain  the skills that I want or experiences  that I want for me to get to my next  level and depending on where I am in my  career sometimes I don't know where like  the goal is per se I don't exactly know  what is the destination of where I want  to get to in my career but even if I  don't know that answer I know kind of  what are some of the skills that I want  to learn what are the incremental steps  that will help me at least uncover what  that goal is or get me to I don't know  be excited about my work or what are  some things that I'm just really curious  about learning or wanting to know or  build upon and just to spell out like  what I mean by opportunity it's things  like am I going to have the chance to  work on a project that'll help me learn  X Y &amp; Z skill skills like technical  skills architectural skills speaking  skills communication skills  collaboration skills organization skills  like there's so many things there it's  also about the people who are there so  who are the people who are going to  teach me these skills who are people who  can mentor me and help me uncover what  my next goal in my career is going to be  what does their network look like who  can they introduce me to to do that if  they don't have those skills and these  are things that when I go to an  interview with a company I ask a lot of  questions about this  I try to meet as many people as possible  to get a sense of what are these  opportunities that exist here at this  company I also have conversations with  like my existing manager and existing  mentors to talk about hey I have these  things that I want to learn what are  some things that I could do to learn  these things  what are perspectives that I don't have  that you think would be helpful for me  to get to this next level so that's  number two what opportunities or doors  or next rungs of jungle tombs does this  opportunity reveal for me in the last  point which I personally think is the  most important thing for me when I'm  making a career decision is am I excited  about this I'm someone who really likes  trusting my gut I think I'm very honest  to myself when I do that and even though  it feels a logical your instinct is  developed by lots of little decisions  and experiences that you've had in the  past that maybe you can't tie them back  to you therefore it feels illogical but  I think it's an important thing to  really listen to yourself to say hey is  this something that just like genuinely  excites me is this something that gets  my heart rate up am I really excited to  like go after and do this thing does it  make me a little bit nervous because I'm  going out of my comfort zone but  ultimately is it going to be a good  thing for me these are really really  important questions that I ask myself  like I remember when I was applying to  patreon when I first saw the job posting  I remember just feeling this sense of  like I have to have this job because I  love this so much and I made my resume  and cover letter and day and I was just  like this is this is gonna happen just  go for it and those moments are really  special and I think it's a really cool  thing to experience and when you feel  that about an opportunity most of the  time it's going to be the right thing to  pursue so those are the three things  that I consider when making a career  decision in tech number one are my needs  met number two what opportunities is  this open up for me and number three am  i genuinely excited about this if you  have any other things that you consider  when you are making a career  decision then leave it in the comments  down below I think a lot of us consider  a lot of different things and depending  who you are you may weigh some things  heavier than the other and that's  totally normal we all have different  needs and different lives and different  priorities in life but those are the  ways that I find have been really  helpful for me in making my decisions  and I hope that they are helpful for you  too like I said be sure to check out the  podcast because we talked about  decisions literally decision making in  if/else so don't forget to check that  out in the description box down below  there really so special and well made  and I am so excited about this so I  really hope you enjoy them too if you'd  like to learn more about decisions so  that's it for today if you like this  video please give it a thumbs up and  don't forget to subscribe to my channel  and hit the notification bell I've got a  lot of videos coming out before the end  of the year so if you'd like to be  notified of them when they come out  please do that I would be so grateful to  know who is a part of this community and  that you like my stuff and that you want  to watch more of my videos alright well  that's it I hope you all have a great  day wherever you are bye  [Music]
 

